{"headers":{"content-type":"text/event-stream; charset=utf-8","cache-control":"no-cache"},"model":"claude-opus-5","prompt":"user: <system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# currentDate\nToday's date is 2026-08-28.\n\n      IMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>\n\n\nmówimy po polsku\nsystem: Available agent types for the Agent tool:\n- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)\n- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI, early-access enablement) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Bash, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)\n- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)\n- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)\n\nWhen you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\n\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- 101-agents-md: Create or surgically update a repo's agent onboarding doc (AGENTS.md, \"Repository Guidelines\"). Use when the user asks to \"create AGENTS.md\", wants an \"agent onboarding doc\", a \"contributor guide for agents\", or a refresh of an existing agent guide.\n- 101-archive: Close a completed change — stamp change.md as archived, move the folder into context/archive/, commit. Use AFTER /101-impl-review. Trigger phrases -- \"archive the change\", \"close this change\", \"zamknij zmianę\".\n- 101-bootstrapper: Scaffold a project into the current directory from the context/foundation/tech-stack.md hand-off, always preserving context/. Use when the user says \"bootstrap the project\", \"scaffold the app\", \"postaw projekt\". Use AFTER /101-tech-stack-selector.\n- 101-e2e: Add real-browser tests to a change — ONE verified test per risk, never a surface sweep. Drives a plan's browser-level phases or a single named risk; needs the feature built. Trigger phrases -- \"e2e\", \"browser test\", \"Playwright test\", \"test przeglądarkowy\".\n- 101-frame: Challenge framing assumptions about WHAT the problem is before planning HOW — when an observation and its stated cause arrive bundled, or scope/design is questioned. Triggers -- \"fix\", \"bug\", \"root cause\", \"should we even\", \"is this the right\", \"rethink\", \"przemyśl zakres\". Use BEFORE /101-plan.\n- 101-goal-implement: Autonomously execute an approved plan from context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md with zero human interaction — the unattended sibling of /101-implement for goal-mode and headless (claude -p) runs. Trigger phrases -- \"run the plan unattended\", \"autonomous implementation\", \"headless\".\n- 101-health-check: Operational readiness audit of an existing project — dependencies, tests, CI, config; writes context/foundation/health-check.md with an impact-ordered fix list. Trigger phrases -- \"health check\", \"audit my project\", \"audyt projektu\". Use AFTER /101-stack-assess, BEFORE /101-agents-md.\n- 101-impl-review: Review the implementation against its plan — drift, risky decisions, pattern breaks — then triage each finding interactively. Use AFTER /101-implement (or /101-tdd, /101-e2e), BEFORE /101-archive. Invoke as /101-impl-review <change-id|plan-path> [phase N] for a fresh run, or with a saved-review path to resume triage. Trigger phrases -- \"review the implementation\", \"impl review\", \"oceń implementację\".\n- 101-impl-review-ci: Run the implementation-vs-plan review non-interactively on a PR in CI — the pipeline sibling of /101-impl-review; commits the report and posts line + summary comments. Use when the request mentions CI, GitHub Actions, automated PR review, or reviewing a PR without a human in the loop.\n- 101-implement: Execute an approved plan from context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md phase by phase, with a confirmation gate and a commit per phase. Use when the user says \"implement the plan\", \"execute the plan\", \"wdróż plan\", or names a change to implement. Use AFTER /101-plan.\n- 101-infra-research: Recommend a deployment platform via a constraint interview plus bias-checked web research; writes context/foundation/infrastructure.md with a scored comparison. Trigger phrases -- \"where should I deploy\", \"infra research\", \"wybierz platformę\". Use AFTER /101-prd or /101-tech-stack-selector.\n- 101-init: Scaffold the context directory skeleton — context/{changes,archive,foundation}/ with convention READMEs — creating only what is missing. Trigger phrases -- \"init the context\", \"set up the project skeleton\", \"zainicjuj kontekst\". Optional; run once, BEFORE /101-shape or /101-new.\n- 101-lesson: Append ONE recurring rule to context/foundation/lessons.md — the learning loop the toolkit reads at start. Trigger phrases -- \"save a lesson\", \"add a recurring rule\", \"zapisz lekcję\".\n- 101-mom-test: Check whether a problem is real BEFORE anyone builds a solution — non-leading critique, interview script, and go/no-go thresholds grounded in past behavior. Trigger phrases -- \"mom test\", \"is this problem real\", \"walidacja problemu\". Use AFTER /101-opportunity-map, BEFORE /101-shape.\n- 101-new: Open a new change folder under context/changes/<change-id> with a change.md identity file; all work on the change lives there until archived. Trigger phrases -- \"new change\", \"open a change\", \"nowa zmiana\".\n- 101-opportunity-map: Turn recurring frictions or unmet needs into a build / buy / complement / wait decision artifact with at most one recommended candidate. Use at the very start, before shaping or validation (\"is this worth building\", \"mapa okazji\"). Use BEFORE /101-mom-test and /101-shape.\n- 101-pack-init: Package a custom AI toolkit (skills + rules + installer) as a versioned npm package skeleton — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core. Trigger phrases -- \"package my toolkit\", \"make my skills installable\", \"spakuj mój toolkit\". First link of the trio — pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry.\n- 101-plan: Turn a task plus existing frame/research artifacts into a complete implementation plan with NO open questions at context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md. Trigger phrases -- \"plan this change\", \"create implementation plan\", \"zaplanuj zmianę\". Use AFTER /101-new, BEFORE /101-implement.\n- 101-plan-review: Review an implementation plan for substance, feasibility, and architectural fitness BEFORE any code exists; produces a 5-dimension scorecard with triage. Trigger phrases -- \"review this plan\", \"is this plan good\", \"oceń plan\". Use AFTER /101-plan, BEFORE /101-implement.\n- 101-prd: Generate context/foundation/prd.md from shape-notes (or raw notes) against the locked PRD schema; auto-routes greenfield vs brownfield. Use AFTER /101-shape, not in place of it. Trigger phrases -- \"write the PRD\", \"generate PRD\", \"stwórz PRD\".\n- 101-research: Answer a research question about the existing system before planning; produces context/changes/<change-id>/research.md with code references. Trigger phrases -- \"research the codebase\", \"how does X work here\", \"zbadaj kod\". Use BEFORE /101-plan when the system is unfamiliar.\n- 101-roadmap: Generate context/foundation/roadmap.md from a PRD as ordered vertical end-to-end slices — user-visible milestones. Use AFTER /101-prd; NOT for per-change planning (that's /101-plan). Trigger phrases -- \"generate roadmap\", \"what should I build first\", \"stwórz roadmapę\".\n- 101-rule-review: Review the condition of an AI rules file (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .cursor/rules/*.mdc, any target tool); produces a five-check scorecard with concrete fixes — grades the artifact, never the decisions in it. Trigger phrases -- \"review AI rules\", \"audit AGENTS.md\", \"check my CLAUDE.md\".\n- 101-setup-cicd: Generate the validation + publish pipeline for a packaged AI toolkit — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core. Trigger phrases -- \"publish pipeline for the toolkit\", \"CI for my toolkit package\", \"potok publikacji\". Second link of the trio — pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry.\n- 101-shape: Facilitate a structured discovery conversation turning an idea — greenfield or brownfield, auto-detected — into context/foundation/shape-notes.md. Use BEFORE /101-prd, not in place of it. Trigger phrases -- \"new project\", \"I have an idea\", \"od pomysłu\", \"greenfield\", \"brownfield\".\n- 101-stack-assess: Assess an existing project's stack for agent-friendliness against the four quality gates; writes context/foundation/stack-assessment.md. Trigger phrases -- \"assess my stack\", \"is my stack agent-friendly\", \"oceń mój stack\". Use AFTER /101-prd (brownfield), BEFORE /101-health-check.\n- 101-tdd: Drive an approved plan's TDD-able phases test-first through red→green→refactor; everything else routes to /101-implement. Use when the user says \"tdd\", \"test-first\", \"red green refactor\", or wants a plan phase driven by tests.\n- 101-tech-stack-selector: Pick a starter and stack for a greenfield project after the PRD is written — reads context/foundation/prd.md, writes the context/foundation/tech-stack.md hand-off. Trigger phrases -- \"what stack should I use\", \"pick a stack\", \"co wybrać do projektu\". Use AFTER /101-prd, BEFORE /101-bootstrapper.\n- 101-test-plan: Stateful, phased test-rollout orchestrator for existing products — writes context/foundation/test-plan.md, then drives each phase through the change lifecycle. Trigger phrases -- \"create test plan\", \"test strategy\", \"strategia testów\". Use AFTER /101-prd and /101-roadmap. Brownfield only.\n- 101-tf-registry: Generate Terraform for a private npm registry (AWS CodeArtifact-shaped) hosting a packaged AI toolkit — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core, optional last link of the trio (pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry). Trigger phrases -- \"private npm registry terraform\", \"prywatny rejestr\".\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\nassistant: Jasne, mówimy po polsku. W czym mogę pomóc?\nuser: <command-message>101-init</command-message>\n<command-name>/101-init</command-name>\n\nBase directory for this skill: /course/.claude/skills/101-init\n\n# 101-init — Context directory scaffold\n\nCreates the directories where the rest of the 101 set stores its artifacts,\nplus the load-bearing-names registry. Autonomy: writes files without asking,\nbut ONLY files that do not exist yet.\n\nInvocation: `/101-init` — no arguments; everything the run needs comes\nfrom the repository state.\n\n**This is NOT a precondition.** Every other 101 skill bootstraps its own\nfiles when it needs them. This skill exists purely for people who prefer to\nlay out the skeleton up front. Say this out loud when the user seems to\nbelieve running it is mandatory — the belief grows on its own otherwise.\n\n## The seven entities\n\nEach entity is handled **independently** — per-artifact idempotency, not\nall-or-nothing. A partially prepared repository is completed without\ntouching what already exists; a fully prepared one gets nothing but the\nstate printout.\n\n1. `context/changes/` — in-flight units of work, one folder per change.\n2. `context/archive/` — completed changes, moved here when archived.\n3. `context/foundation/` — living cross-change documents.\n4. `context/changes/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n5. `context/archive/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n6. `context/foundation/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n7. `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` — registry of load-bearing names\n   (create `docs/reference/` on the way if needed).\n\n**Never overwrite existing content.** An existing README or registry stays\nuntouched even when its content differs from the canonical text below —\nsomeone may have adapted it deliberately, and this skill has no authority\nto revert that.\n\n## README contents (write only when the file is absent)\n\n`context/changes/README.md` — say that this holds in-flight changes, one\nfolder per change at `context/changes/<change-id>/`, identified by a\n`change.md` identity file and opened with `/101-new`; that a change folder\ngathers everything change-scoped (research, frame, plan, reviews); and that\na finished change is moved under `context/archive/` with `/101-archive`.\n\n`context/archive/README.md` — say that this holds completed changes moved\nfrom `context/changes/` by `/101-archive`; that it is read-only by\nconvention; and that 101 skills refuse to write here (G-14).\n\n`context/foundation/README.md` — say that this holds living documents that\nspan multiple changes (each project picks which it needs — requirements,\ntech stack, roadmap, lessons, test plan), owned by the skills that read and\nwrite them, plus three conventions:\n- **Edit-in-place** (G-05a): when a foundation doc changes incrementally,\n  edit the existing file — never create dated just-in-case copies.\n- **Archive only when superseded**: a doc replaced by a new approach (not\n  merely refined) moves to `foundation/archive/YYYY-MM-DD-<doc>.md` and the\n  replacement takes the original path; nothing reads the archive routinely.\n- **Named anti-pattern — change-scoped doc in foundation**: anything tied\n  to a single change (its plan, research, review) belongs under\n  `context/changes/<change-id>/`, never here. Foundation is for what\n  outlives any one change.\n\n## Contract-surfaces registry (write only when absent)\n\n`docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` starts as an **empty registry** of\nload-bearing names — the identifiers other code, tools or people depend on\nstaying stable. Give it a short intro explaining:\n- what belongs here: data **structures**, **routes/endpoints**, file and\n  message **formats**, **events** — one `##` heading per surface, with a\n  line or two on who depends on it;\n- how it is consumed: `/101-plan-review` pattern-scans plan text against\n  this file's H2 headings and flags plans that touch a registered surface\n  without acknowledging it. When the file is absent, that check is\n  **silently skipped** — so an empty-but-present registry is already\n  useful the day the first surface is added;\n- who owns what: **this skill owns the scaffold** — file creation and the\n  header/intro; **the user owns the entries** (any skill may add one, but\n  only on the user's request); **`/101-plan-review` is a read-only\n  consumer** and never writes here.\n\n## Ownership boundary — files this skill does NOT create\n\nDocuments with a dedicated owner are bootstrapped by that owner on first\nuse, never here. In particular:\n- `context/foundation/lessons.md` → owned by `/101-lesson`;\n- the starter registry → maintained with the toolkit\n  (`101-tech-stack-selector/references/`).\nThis keeps the responsibility line clean: 101-init owns the skeleton,\nowners own their documents.\n\nThe one deliberate exception is `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` —\nand it is an exception in appearance only: 101-init owns that file's\nscaffold (creation plus header), the user owns its entries, and\n`/101-plan-review` merely reads it. Scaffolding it here therefore creates\nnothing another skill owns.\n\n## State printout (fixed form)\n\nAfter processing all seven entities, print exactly one line per entity in\nthe order above, each marked **created** or **existed**, followed by a\nshort recap of what each of the three directories is for. Same form every\nrun — a re-run on a complete skeleton produces only\nthis printout. Mirror the user's language in the printout (G-11); keep the\nentity numbering internal (G-10).\n\n## Closing\n\nInit is a standalone convenience, not the first link of a mandatory\nchain — so the closing does NOT push a next command. State briefly what\nexists now: the three directories, their READMEs, the registry — each\ncreated or already present. Only when the user has already said they are\nstarting a project, add a single pointer to `/101-shape` as the natural\nnext step. Otherwise recommend nothing; never run anything yourself.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- Never overwrites existing content, even content that differs from\n  canonical.\n- Each of the seven entities is created independently; partial state is\n  completed without touching what exists.\n- Not a precondition for anything — other skills self-bootstrap; declare\n  this when relevant.\n- Never creates documents owned by other skills. The contract-surfaces\n  registry is not a violation of this: 101-init owns its scaffold, the\n  user owns its entries, `/101-plan-review` only reads it.\n- Never auto-runs a follow-up skill, and never pushes a command chain at\n  close.\n\n## Thresholds\n\n- Seven independent entities (three directories, three READMEs, one\n  registry).\n\n## Touchpoints\n\n- Creates the space every other 101 skill writes into.\n- `/101-shape` checks for `context/foundation/` and may offer to delegate\n  here when it is missing.\n- `/101-new` requires `context/changes/` and points here when absent.\n- `/101-plan-review` reads `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` when\n  present; its absence silently disables that one check.","recorded":"2026-08-28T11:54:03.139Z"}
